# /data/wittypi-schedule.env — the duty cycle. Two integers, both in seconds.
#
# Copy this to /data/wittypi-schedule.env and edit the two values. An ABSENT
# file is the supported default: hand-scheduled mode, where nothing renews the
# alarms and wake-guard's shutdown rescue is the only automatic arming.
#
# ── THE ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND ────────────────────────────────────────────
# You set two DURATIONS, not two times. The scheduler anchors windows on
# absolute cycle phase since the Unix epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC), so:
#
#     CYCLE = ON + OFF
#     wake  ≡ 0  (mod CYCLE)      the node powers ON at these instants
#     stop  = wake + ON           and powers OFF ON seconds later
#
# If CYCLE divides 86400 evenly, the windows land on the SAME UTC wall-clock
# times every day, forever. That is why the examples below all divide a day.
#
# ⚠️ THERE IS NO START-TIME SETTING, and no phase offset. Do not go looking
# for one. Change ON to change window LENGTH; change CYCLE to change how many
# windows a day you get and therefore where they fall. That is the whole
# control surface, and its smallness is deliberate: the moment appointments
# are measured from "when the script ran" rather than from the clock, every
# repair path (the .path unit, the supervisor's retry) silently moves your
# duty cycle instead of restoring it.
#
# ── WORKED EXAMPLES — pick one, paste it, delete the rest ──────────────────
#
#   Two windows a day, 3h each  (CYCLE 12h)   00:00-03:00 and 12:00-15:00 UTC
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_ON_SEC=10800
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_OFF_SEC=32400
#
#   Two windows a day, 4h each  (CYCLE 12h)   00:00-04:00 and 12:00-16:00 UTC
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_ON_SEC=14400
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_OFF_SEC=28800
#
#   One window a day, 3h        (CYCLE 24h)   00:00-03:00 UTC
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_ON_SEC=10800
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_OFF_SEC=75600
#
#   Four windows a day, 1h each (CYCLE 6h)    00:00, 06:00, 12:00, 18:00 UTC
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_ON_SEC=3600
#       WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_OFF_SEC=18000
#
# All times UTC — set WITTYPI_TZ_NOTE in /data/wittypi.env and the tools will
# print your offsets next to every timestamp they render.
#
# ── THE TWO BOUNDS (both enforced in code; a breach is a refusal) ──────────
#   ON  >= T_MARKGOOD + 60      from the shipped /usr/lib/site/timing-terms.
#                               312 + 60 = 372s on a Pi Zero. A shorter window
#                               shuts the node down before the boot is marked
#                               healthy, and repeated cycles condemn a good
#                               A/B slot for a fault in neither.
#   ON + OFF <= reg49 - 3600    guaranteed wake (26h by default) is the
#                               last-resort backstop and must outlast the
#                               schedule it backs up. Read LIVE from the
#                               register, not from a file.
#
# ── APPLYING IT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Write the file. wittypi-schedule.path notices and re-arms within seconds —
# no reboot needed. Then ALWAYS read it back:
#
#     wittypi-audit
#
# The failure you must not miss is "file written, scheduler refused": the node
# then looks configured while the registers still hold whatever they held
# before. wittypi-audit prints exactly that as
#     !! configured but alarm2 holds NO shutdown appointment
#
# ⚠️ Requires WITTYPI_GUARANTEED_WAKE to be set and applied in
# /data/wittypi.env first — the scheduler REFUSES outright if register 49
# reads 0. Check with `wittypi check`.
#
# ── YOU CANNOT BRICK IT FROM HERE ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Every failure direction pushes the node UP, never dark. A bad value refuses
# and writes nothing. An unreadable RTC refuses. If the wake cannot be armed,
# the shutdown is deliberately not attempted. Any shutdown with nothing armed
# gets wake-guard's +60min rescue, and guaranteed wake backs up even that.
# The worst realistic outcome of a typo here is a node that stays awake.

WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_ON_SEC=10800
WITTYPI_SCHEDULE_OFF_SEC=32400
